For {う}-verbs (godan verbs), move the last sound from the u-row to the e-row of the kana chart and add {る}:

- {書[か]く} → {書[か]ける}, {泳[およ]ぐ} → {泳[およ]げる}
- {話[はな]す} → {話[はな]せる}, {待[ま]つ} → {待[ま]てる}
- {遊[あそ]ぶ} → {遊[あそ]べる}, {読[よ]む} → {読[よ]める}
- {帰[かえ]る} → {帰[かえ]れる}, {買[か]う} → {買[か]える}

The result is a {る}-verb: {書[か]けます}, {書[か]けない}, {書[か]けた}.

Watch out for {う}-verbs that happen to end in {る}, such as {帰[かえ]る}, {入[はい]る}, {走[はし]る} and {知[し]る}. They follow the {う}-verb rule, so it's {帰[かえ]れる}, never {帰[かえ]られる}. If you're unsure which group a verb is in, check its {ます}-form: {帰[かえ]ります} has {り}, so it's a {う}-verb.

The volitional form is the plain, casual version of {〜ましょう}. Use it with friends and family to suggest doing something together: {一緒[いっしょ]に帰[かえ]ろう} 'Let's go home together'. Said to yourself, it shows a decision made on the spot: {よし、がんばろう} 'Right, I'll give it my best'.

How to make it:

- {る}-verbs: drop {る}, add {よう}: {食[た]べる} → {食[た]べよう}, {寝[ね]る} → {寝[ね]よう}
- {う}-verbs: move the last sound to the o-row and add {う}: {書[か]く} → {書[か]こう}, {話[はな]す} → {話[はな]そう}, {待[ま]つ} → {待[ま]とう}, {飲[の]む} → {飲[の]もう}, {帰[かえ]る} → {帰[かえ]ろう}, {買[か]う} → {買[か]おう}
- Irregular: {する} → {しよう}, {来[く]る} → {来[こ]よう}

Add {か} to make a casual offer or suggestion, like {〜ましょうか}: {手伝[てつだ]おうか} 'Shall I help?'

Many Japanese verbs come in pairs. A **transitive** verb has someone acting on an object marked with {を}: {私[わたし]がドアを開[あ]けた} 'I opened the door.' An **intransitive** verb describes the thing changing by itself, marked with {が}: {ドアが開[あ]いた} 'The door opened.'

Common pairs (transitive / intransitive):

- {開[あ]ける} / {開[あ]く}, {閉[し]める} / {閉[し]まる}
- {始[はじ]める} / {始[はじ]まる}, {止[と]める} / {止[と]まる}
- {つける} / {つく}, {消[け]す} / {消[き]える}
- {入[い]れる} / {入[はい]る}, {出[だ]す} / {出[で]る}
- {落[お]とす} / {落[お]ちる}, {壊[こわ]す} / {壊[こわ]れる}

Japanese often prefers the intransitive when describing what happened without focusing on who did it, which can sound less like blaming: {コップが割[わ]れた} 'The glass broke.'

{ば} focuses on the **condition** that leads to a result: 'If (only) you do A, B will follow.' It's typical for general rules, advice and hypotheticals.

How to make it:

- {う}-verbs: last sound to the e-row + {ば}: {書[か]けば}, {話[はな]せば}, {待[ま]てば}, {飲[の]めば}, {帰[かえ]れば}, {買[か]えば}
- {る}-verbs: drop {る}, add {れば}: {食[た]べれば}, {見[み]れば}
- {する} → {すれば}, {来[く]る} → {来[く]れば}
- {い}-adjectives: {安[やす]い} → {安[やす]ければ}, {いい} → {よければ}; negative {〜ない} → {〜なければ}
- {な}-adjectives and nouns: {静[しず]かなら}, {学生[がくせい]なら}

One restriction: if the {ば} part is an action and the main clause is a request, invitation or intention by the same person, use {たら} instead. {駅[えき]に着[つ]けば電話[でんわ]してください} sounds wrong. With states ({ある}, {いる}, adjectives, potential verbs) it's fine: {時間[じかん]があれば、来[き]てください}.

In conversation the long 'must' forms are almost always shortened:

- {〜なければ} → {〜なきゃ}: {行[い]かなきゃ}
- {〜なくては} → {〜なくちゃ}: {行[い]かなくちゃ}
- {〜ないと(いけない)}: {行[い]かないと}

Very often the ending ({いけない} / {ならない}) is dropped completely, leaving just {もう行[い]かなきゃ} 'I've gotta go.' These dropped versions are casual.

{〜ないといけません} is the one you can use in polite conversation too; it's softer than {〜なければなりません} and very common at work: {確認[かくにん]しないといけませんね} 'We'll need to check that.'

{ても} means 'even if' or 'even though': the result in the main clause doesn't change, despite what you'd expect. {雨[あめ]が降[ふ]っても、行[い]きます} 'Even if it rains, I'll go.'

Forms:

- Verbs: {て}-form + {も}: {降[ふ]っても}, {食[た]べても}, {飲[の]んでも}
- {い}-adjectives: {〜くても}: {高[たか]くても}
- {な}-adjectives and nouns: {〜でも}: {静[しず]かでも}, {子[こ]どもでも}
- Negative: {〜なくても}

With question words it means 'no matter how / what / how many times': {いくら食[た]べても} 'no matter how much I eat', {何回[なんかい]聞[き]いても} 'however many times I listen', {どんなに忙[いそが]しくても} 'however busy'.

It works for real past events too: {薬[くすり]を飲[の]んでも、治[なお]らなかった} 'Even though I took medicine, it didn't get better.'

This {そう} gives your impression from what you can **see right now**, before you know for sure. 'It looks ~.'

- {い}-adjectives: drop {い}: {おいしい} → {おいしそう} 'looks tasty', {高[たか]い} → {高[たか]そう}
- {な}-adjectives: stem + {そう}: {元気[げんき]そう} 'looks well', {丈夫[じょうぶ]そう}
- Verbs: {ます}-stem + {そう} means 'looks like it's about to ~': {雨[あめ]が降[ふ]りそうだ} 'It looks like it's going to rain', {落[お]ちそう} 'looks like it'll fall'
- Irregular: {いい} → {よさそう}, {ない} → {なさそう}; negatives: {おいしくなさそう}

{そう} works like a {な}-adjective: {おいしそうなケーキ} 'a delicious-looking cake', {楽[たの]しそうに遊[あそ]ぶ} 'play happily (looking like they're having fun)'.

It's not used for things obvious at a glance, like colours: say {赤[あか]い}, not {赤[あか]そう}.

This second {そうだ} reports information you got from somewhere else: a person, the news, a forecast. 'I hear that ~ / Apparently ~.' {明日[あした]は雪[ゆき]が降[ふ]るそうです} 'I hear it's going to snow tomorrow.'

The big difference from 'looks like' {そう} is the connection: hearsay {そうだ} attaches to the **full plain form**, including {だ} and past tense:

- {降[ふ]るそうだ}, {降[ふ]ったそうだ}, {降[ふ]らないそうだ}
- {おいしいそうだ}, {静[しず]かだそうだ}, {学生[がくせい]だそうだ}

So {おいしそう} = 'looks tasty' but {おいしいそうだ} = 'I hear it's tasty'.

Name the source with {〜によると} or {〜の話[はなし]では}. Hearsay {そうだ} itself doesn't go into the past or negative; change what's inside instead.

The causative means someone **makes** (or lets) another person do something. The causer is the subject.

How to make it:

- {る}-verbs: drop {る}, add {させる}: {食[た]べさせる}, {見[み]させる}
- {う}-verbs: last sound to the a-row + {せる}: {書[か]かせる}, {待[ま]たせる}, {飲[の]ませる}, {帰[かえ]らせる}; {言[い]う} → {言[い]わせる}
- {する} → {させる}, {来[く]る} → {来[こ]させる}

Particles for the person who is made to act:

- If the verb already has an object with {を}, the person takes {に}: {子[こ]どもに野菜[やさい]を食[た]べさせる} 'make the child eat vegetables.'
- With intransitive verbs, the person usually takes {を}: {部下[ぶか]を出張[しゅっちょう]させる} 'send a subordinate on a business trip.'

It also covers causing emotions: {母[はは]を心配[しんぱい]させる} 'make my mother worry'. And the everyday apology {お待[ま]たせしました} / {待[ま]たせてごめん} 'Sorry to keep you waiting' uses it too.

Combine causative and passive and you get 'be made to do': {私[わたし]は母[はは]に野菜[やさい]を食[た]べさせられた} 'I was made to eat vegetables by my mother.' The subject is the person who had to do it, and the one who forced them is marked with {に}. It always carries a feeling of reluctance.

How to make it: take the causative ({させる}) and make it passive ({させられる}).

- {る}-verbs: {食[た]べさせられる}, {覚[おぼ]えさせられる}
- {う}-verbs: the full form is {書[か]かせられる}, but the short form {書[か]かされる} is far more common: {飲[の]まされる}, {待[ま]たされる}, {行[い]かされる}. Exception: verbs ending in {す} only use the long form: {話[はな]させられる}.
- {する} → {させられる}, {来[く]る} → {来[こ]させられる}

You'll hear this a lot when people complain about school, parents and work.

{すぎる} means 'to go past', so attached to a verb or adjective it means 'too much / too ~'.

- Verbs: {ます}-stem + {すぎる}: {食[た]べすぎる} 'overeat', {飲[の]みすぎる} 'drink too much', {働[はたら]きすぎる}
- {い}-adjectives: drop {い}: {高[たか]すぎる} 'too expensive', {大[おお]きすぎる}
- {な}-adjectives: stem + {すぎる}: {静[しず]かすぎる}, {簡単[かんたん]すぎる}
- Irregular: {いい} → {よすぎる}; {ない} → {なさすぎる}

{すぎる} is a {る}-verb, so it conjugates normally: {食[た]べすぎた}, {高[たか]すぎます}, {多[おお]すぎて}. The stem {すぎ} works as a noun: {飲[の]みすぎに注意[ちゅうい]} 'beware of drinking too much'.

It usually implies something negative — 'more than is good'.

Some very common verbs have their own special respectful forms. Use them for the actions of customers, superiors, teachers and anyone you want to show respect to:

- {いる}, {行[い]く}, {来[く]る} → {いらっしゃる}
- {言[い]う} → {おっしゃる}
- {食[た]べる}, {飲[の]む} → {召[め]し上[あ]がる}
- {する} → {なさる}
- {見[み]る} → {ご覧[らん]になる}
- {知[し]っている} → {ご存[ぞん]じだ}
- {くれる} → {くださる}

Watch the {ます}-forms of {いらっしゃる}, {おっしゃる}, {なさる} and {くださる}: the {る} becomes {い}, not {り}. So {いらっしゃいます}, {おっしゃいます}, {なさいます}, {くださいます}.

You'll hear these in everyday phrases: {いらっしゃいませ} 'Welcome (to our shop)', {どうぞ召[め]し上[あ]がってください} 'Please, help yourself', {ご存[ぞん]じですか} 'Do you know?'

Humble verbs lower **your own** actions (or your group's) to show respect to the listener. The most useful ones:

- {行[い]く}, {来[く]る} → {参[まい]る}
- {いる} → {おる}; so {〜ている} → {〜ております}
- {言[い]う} → {申[もう]す}: {ジョンと申[もう]します} 'My name is John'
- {する} → {いたす}
- {食[た]べる}, {飲[の]む}, {もらう} → {いただく}
- {見[み]る} → {拝見[はいけん]する}
- {訪[たず]ねる} (visit), {聞[き]く} (ask) → {伺[うかが]う}
- {知[し]っている} → {存[ぞん]じている}
- {あげる} → {さしあげる}

In practice, {〜と申[もう]します} and {〜ております} are the ones you'll use from day one: in self-introductions, on the phone, and in work emails.

Never use these for the other person's actions. {先生[せんせい]が参[まい]りました} would be a mistake; the teacher {いらっしゃいました}.
